<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbadid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1023–<a href="/years/1091">1091</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Abbadids, Banu Abbad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abbadids were an Arab dynasty that ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> from 1023 to 1091. Under al-Mutadid and his son al-Mutamid, a celebrated poet, they absorbed neighbouring taifas across the south-west and made Seville the strongest Muslim state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. Pressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, al-Mutamid called in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>, who deposed him in 1091.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Abbadid</b> ruler al-Mutadid of the Taifa of Seville annexed Arcos in the 1050s or 1060s as he swept up the Berber principalities of the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-arcos">Taifa of Arcos</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1051 the <b>Abbadid</b> ruler al-Mutadid of Seville forced the last ruler, Abd al-Aziz al-Bakri, to give up Huelva and Saltés.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-huelva">Taifa of Huelva</a></li><li><blockquote>He ruled in the name of a shadowy pretender, supposedly the vanished caliph Hisham II, which gave the <b>Abbadids</b> a claim to wider authority.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-seville">Taifa of Seville</a></li></ul></section>
